const ERROR_TYPE_TABLE = `| Error Type | Explanation |
|---|---|
| Misunderstanding | The reviewer misinterprets claims or ideas presented in the paper, leading to inaccurate or irrelevant comments. |
| Neglect | The reviewer overlooks important details explicitly stated in the paper, resulting in unwarranted questions or critiques. |
| Vague Critique | The review lacks specificity, claiming missing components without clearly identifying what is missing. |
| Out-of-scope | The reviewer suggests additional methods, experiments, or analyses that are beyond the intended scope of the paper. |
| Invalid Criticism | The reviewer's criticism is considered invalid, especially when suggesting impractical experiments or trivializing results. |
| Superficial Review | The reviewer appears to have only skimmed the paper, providing generic or unsupported comments about the presence or absence of weaknesses. |
| Unstated statement | Statements made in the review are not supported by content in the paper. |
| Excessive demands | if the weaknesses are just asking for excessive things that are not necessary for a good paper. |
| Generic comment | weaknesses are just generic comments that can apply to any paper, without really pointing out the specific problems of the paper. |`

const GUIDELINE = "# Weakness Reliability Guideline\n\nUse this guideline to judge whether a weakness raised in a paper review is RELIABLE (well-grounded, paper-specific, actionable) or UNRELIABLE (matching one of the error patterns below).\n\n## Error type table\n\n| Error Type | Explanation |\n|---|---|\n| Misunderstanding | The reviewer misinterprets claims or ideas presented in the paper, leading to inaccurate or irrelevant comments. |\n| Neglect | The reviewer overlooks important details explicitly stated in the paper, resulting in unwarranted questions or critiques. |\n| Vague Critique | The review lacks specificity, claiming missing components without clearly identifying what is missing. |\n| Out-of-scope | The reviewer suggests additional methods, experiments, or analyses that are beyond the intended scope of the paper. |\n| Invalid Criticism | The reviewer's criticism is considered invalid, especially when suggesting impractical experiments or trivializing results. |\n| Superficial Review | The reviewer appears to have only skimmed the paper, providing generic or unsupported comments about the presence or absence of weaknesses. |\n| Unstated statement | Statements made in the review are not supported by content in the paper. |\n| Excessive demands | if the weaknesses are just asking for excessive things that are not necessary for a good paper. |\n| Generic comment | weaknesses are just generic comments that can apply to any paper, without really pointing out the specific problems of the paper. |\n\n## Unreliable weakness examples (per error type)\n\nEach example is a real reviewer segment annotated by a human as UNRELIABLE, with the human's explanation of why. Do NOT assume a weakness is unreliable just because it is a criticism -- only mark unreliable when it matches one of these error patterns.\n\n### Misunderstanding\n\n*The reviewer misinterprets claims or ideas presented in the paper, leading to inaccurate or irrelevant comments.*\n\n**Example 1**\n> Reviewer wrote: But the core technical contribution is just an LM-based reweighting. Methodological nvoelty is a shortage.\n\nWhy unreliable: The method in this paper is not only about LM-based reweighting. For example, it has considered efficient generation of diverse prompts.They are the first to extract knowledge graphs of arbitrary relations from LMs.\n\n**Example 2**\n> Reviewer wrote: I gather that Transformer-XL uses a form of \"top-k\" sampling, that is, a generation mode more restricted than a standard (from the pure probabilistic viewpoint) \"ancestral sampling\". Such restricted sampling (as also beam-search) is known to improve the quality but limit the diversity of the generations. It would be interesting to see if you would obtain the same EBP conclusions with ancestral sampling.\n\nWhy unreliable: The authors do not use ancestral sampling\n\n**Example 3**\n> Reviewer wrote: This provides evidence that finetuning works more robustly than few-shot prompting, and that explicitly training to handle ambiguity helps generalization performance.\n\nWhy unreliable: The authors do not claim that finetuning works more robustly than few-shot prompting.\n\n**Example 4**\n> Reviewer wrote: The fact that this prompting fails on logical tasks (section 6) poses serious concerns on its applicability on harder problems other than simple mathematical operations and, therefore, its real-world utility.\n\nWhy unreliable: The authors never proposed anything universal. they focus on math reasoning only.\n\n**Example 5**\n> Reviewer wrote: Besides, it is well known that meta-learning methods are notoriously hard to tune and can be extremely sensitive to hyperparameter choices. It is hard to tune the framework on a large PLM with a small batch size.\n\nWhy unreliable: For hyperparameter setup, they do not perform per-task tuning but use a fixed set of hyperparameters, which works quite well for all tasks.\n\n**Example 6**\n> Reviewer wrote: It reads like the most significant contribution is using Google search as the information retriever compared with previous work using other search engines on Wikipedia, etc.\n\nWhy unreliable: The major contribution of this work is not using Google search as the retriever. The main contribution of this paper is to leverage the most up-to-date information online, instead of those traditional open-book QA methods. Since the LLM's parametric knowledge and the quick evolution of online knowledge have become more and more popular recently (2023~2024), I think this paper's contribution is also solid at 2021~2022.\n\n**Example 7**\n> Reviewer wrote: It is not clear if the external knowledge used is published or not, and if and how it is possible to obtain it.\n\nWhy unreliable: The method of acquiring external knowledge is not the focus of this paper.\n\n**Example 8**\n> Reviewer wrote: The metric called \u201cprecision@1\u201d should be called \u201caccuracy@1\u201d\n\nWhy unreliable: The authors use precision as the evaluation metric for consistency of previous paper instead of accuracy.\n\n**Example 9**\n> Reviewer wrote: As far as I saw the Figure 2 and 3, the accuracy is heavily reflected by the amount data available during training and some normalization should be needed to compare these metrics each other without biases. Figure 3 also show some remarkable characteristics in translating only into C++, but the paper did not mention about this point.\n\nWhy unreliable: It is not that intuitive to compare the evaluation result to each other in Table 2 given the fact that they belong to different translation directions.\n\n**Example 10**\n> Reviewer wrote: These can happen in practice, but I'm not convinced flipping the label (essentially) is always beneficial especially in rare/infrequent classes settings.\n\nWhy unreliable: The proposed method did not \"flip\" the label in a hard assignment way, instead, they update the weight of soft assignment between the LF and label.\n\n### Neglect\n\n*The reviewer overlooks important details explicitly stated in the paper, resulting in unwarranted questions or critiques.*\n\n**Example 1**\n> Reviewer wrote: But all the tasks in your experiments are evaluated on high-level natural language understanding tasks, which typically require representations at higher layers.\n\nWhy unreliable: Figure 2 shows that the intermediate layer representation is effective on the QNLI and MRPC tasks.\n\n**Example 2**\n> Reviewer wrote: -It is not entirely clear how the shots are defined in the downstream tasks. Are they predefined in the scope of the datasets? This part can be more elaborated.\n\nWhy unreliable: The shots are already defined in Section 4. The reviwer overlooks it.\n\n**Example 3**\n> Reviewer wrote: It will be useful to see results for prompt tuning on other classification or QA tasks, for example from SuperGLUE, where the tasks require quite a different reasoning than LAMA.\n\nWhy unreliable: Similar results were indeed shown on three SuperGLUE tasks in Appendix B.\n\n**Example 4**\n> Reviewer wrote: Is it the performance for a given prompt? If so, that should be explained in the caption.\n\nWhy unreliable: Though the authors didn't mentioned the setting in caption, they did mention it in the paper.\n\n**Example 5**\n> Reviewer wrote: How could the findings benefit the community? The design principle of EncDec-based NMT is the sub-optimal performance/efficiency of LM-based NMT, and this paper just says: \"yes, the intuition is true\", which is not exciting at all.\n\nWhy unreliable: Authors point out that they conduted an analysis that was not done before and LMs are not always better than EncDec based NMT models\n\n**Example 6**\n> Reviewer wrote: Number of shots and classes: the numbers chosen here are a bit arbitrary and more concrete experiments could have been better.\n\nWhy unreliable: This is justified by the authors based on prior work as explanined in 4.3\n\n**Example 7**\n> Reviewer wrote: Usually, the bi-level formulations used in previous work present justifications of why such formulation is adopted, such as meta weight net, MAML, etc. However, no theoretical justification or intuitive explanation is given to explain the necessity and superiority of such a formulation in this paper.\n\nWhy unreliable: The paper provides intuitive motivation in Section 3.2 and Figure 2 for the usage of meta weight and bi-level optimization.\n\n**Example 8**\n> Reviewer wrote: It would be helpful if the author can show how much this will change the model's performance when the labels are correct.\n\nWhy unreliable: The authors show some results when the labels are correct in the third last paragraph of page 6 in the submission.\n\n**Example 9**\n> Reviewer wrote: The paper hints at the design of templates for the UniFew baseline but does not provide enough details for replication.\n\nWhy unreliable: Details about Unifew, including the exact prompt templates used for each task are already included in the appendices C and D (this is mentioned in lines 280-281 in the paper). The author also have included source code to facilitate replication. The reviewer overlooked them.\n\n**Example 10**\n> Reviewer wrote: In other words, the authors further need to whether the improvements over CB (in Table mainly come from by keeping or perfectly memorizing the corpus used for pretraining CB, or whether it comes from the use of new but well-matched corpus that is NOT seen when pretraining CB.\n\nWhy unreliable: This manuscript discusses it in section 5.5.\n\n### Vague Critique\n\n*The review lacks specificity, claiming missing components without clearly identifying what is missing.*\n\n**Example 1**\n> Reviewer wrote: The root cause of this is that the paper did not provide a reasonable criterion about acceptance of this experiment first.\n\nWhy unreliable: This is a vague critique and does not provide details.\n\n**Example 2**\n> Reviewer wrote: The experiment section is weak.\n\nWhy unreliable: Very subjective and vauge. The reviewer should provide explanations.\n\n**Example 3**\n> Reviewer wrote: This paper while taking a step in the right direction, seems a little premature for publication. That being said, the reported results my be of some value after all. It is hard to narrow down on the exact contributions of this paper.\n\nWhy unreliable: The reviewer seems to not provide many concrete ideas on this paper but subjectively says this paper is \"premature for publication\".\n\n**Example 4**\n> Reviewer wrote: _ \"Our leading hypothesis here is that the LSTMs may actually relearn all weights without taking advantage of pretraining\".\n\nWhy unreliable: The reviewer has forgotten to indicate the issue with this sentence from the paper\n\n**Example 5**\n> Reviewer wrote: The setting of using a shorter explanation becomes out of the general hypothesis. This might become a strength if it were presented as a limitation and not as a solution.\n\nWhy unreliable: This statement is vague and difficult to understand. The reviewer should provide reasons why to make this statement.\n\n**Example 6**\n> Reviewer wrote: some grammatical mistakes and typos.\n\nWhy unreliable: The reviewer should provide examples to illustrate the grammatical mistakes and typos.\n\n**Example 7**\n> Reviewer wrote: Individual problematic ingredients are neither being theoretically-proven nor empirically-proven.\n\nWhy unreliable: This is a vague and unreliable comment. The review does not specify what the \"problematic ingredients\" are. The experiments mentioned in this paper are all empirical analysis.\n\n**Example 8**\n> Reviewer wrote: The new findings are not particularly non-trivial.\n\nWhy unreliable: There is not enough justification provided by the reviewer for this claim.\n\n**Example 9**\n> Reviewer wrote: But the novelty of the proposed method is weak.\n\nWhy unreliable: It is unfair to claim weak novelty without offering much justification.\n\n**Example 10**\n> Reviewer wrote: I believe regardless of how good some of transfer learning models perform, we need to elaborate on situations those models collapse.\n\nWhy unreliable: It is not clear why the elaboration on model collapsing is needed\n\n### Out-of-scope\n\n*The reviewer suggests additional methods, experiments, or analyses that are beyond the intended scope of the paper.*\n\n**Example 1**\n> Reviewer wrote: Recently, the Chain-of-Thought prompting (https://arxiv.org/pdf/2201.11903.pdf) has been used by many papers using in-context learning, with reported improvements in model performance (the paper seems to have been cited already 100 times since its release earlier this year). One question this work does not address is whether model sensitivity/variance to retrieved prompts is also present when used in conjunction with chain-of-thought. And whether the vote-k scheme would be useful to limit the number of chain-of-thought annotations needed to improve in-context learning performance.\n\nWhy unreliable: This is a out-of-scope topic for this paper since the experiments are sufficient enough for general in-context learning. This could be a future work.\n\n**Example 2**\n> Reviewer wrote: Lastly, a final concern about real-world utility as the prompting does not perform well on harder problems (logical tasks).\n\nWhy unreliable: This is out of scope for the paper\n\n**Example 3**\n> Reviewer wrote: How does the pre-training (i.e., datasets, model design) of LLMs affect their multilingual abilities?\n\nWhy unreliable: The paper does not involve the pre-training of LLMs. I think this questions is out of the scope of this paper.\n\n**Example 4**\n> Reviewer wrote: The demonstrated examples are all for very basic arithmetic operations, and it is unclear whether this technique can scale to more complicated cases.\n\nWhy unreliable: out of scope for the paper\n\n**Example 5**\n> Reviewer wrote: What is the complexity (time and space) of weak supervision?\n\nWhy unreliable: The \"time and space\"complexity of weak supervision should not be a problem in the scope of this paper.\n\n**Example 6**\n> Reviewer wrote: It is not clear if and how the approach extends to other types of knowledge, outside of the e-commerce and products domain.\n\nWhy unreliable: This is not the focus of this paper\n\n**Example 7**\n> Reviewer wrote: (~) MTPB does not allow for \"going back\" (i.e., a user indicating that a generated response is wrong & clarifying their prompt).\n\nWhy unreliable: This paper focuses on factorizing a complex problem to simple subproblems, the \"going back\" is out of scope\n\n**Example 8**\n> Reviewer wrote: A more systematic analysis is needed to determine the optimal prompts that make the model capture a range of different patterns. This sentence seems to indicate that this will be the focus of the paper, but it isn't my understanding of the purpose of the paper.\n\nWhy unreliable: The reviewer's suggestion is off topic and is out of the scope of this paper.\n\n**Example 9**\n> Reviewer wrote: Since this is an examination work on multiple translation models and configurations, it would have been nice to provide a deeper analysis on the linguistic aspects of the experiments as well: Language pairs with different characteristics (morphologically rich languages for instance) work differently with different models. How different variations of the same model perform on different classes of languages is a significant question to investigate.\n\nWhy unreliable: Already paper has many experiments. Adding this would be out of scope\n\n**Example 10**\n> Reviewer wrote: In fact, this proposed method could be applied in sequence-to-sequence pretraining model, such as mT5. Have you explored this direction? Or leveraging synthetic parallel data generated by the GPT-3 to warm up the previous UNMT models?\n\nWhy unreliable: Out-of-scope. The main focus of this paper is decoder-only LMs.\n\n### Invalid Criticism\n\n*The reviewer's criticism is considered invalid, especially when suggesting impractical experiments or trivializing results.*\n\n**Example 1**\n> Reviewer wrote: 17- Section 7: the author mentions a key difference between T0 and FLAN model is that T0 is an encoder-decoder model which is pretrained and finetuned with different objectives. However, no clear comparison with FLAN model is presented in the paper\n\nWhy unreliable: Flan is a concurrent work, the author of this paper doesn't have the duty to compare T0 with the FLAN.\n\n**Example 2**\n> Reviewer wrote: While it's good to quantify the performance of models on time-stratified datasets, many conclusions are obvious, such as the synthetic experiments on question answering by shifting the end year of training data, as well as the experiments on self-containing reading comprehension.\n\nWhy unreliable: The \"conclusions are obvious\" shouldn't be the weakness.\n\n**Example 3**\n> Reviewer wrote: Results are not particularly surprising\n\nWhy unreliable: This is a invalid criticism. Many findings seem obvious in retrospect, but this does not mean that the community is already aware of them and can use them as building blocks for future work.\n\n**Example 4**\n> Reviewer wrote: There are models that accept more tokens, and some that claim to accept an unbound number (I expect them to deteriorate).\n\nWhy unreliable: But still token limitation is a problem with the free models available at that time.\n\n**Example 5**\n> Reviewer wrote: Another concern regarding the paper that I have is that most of the results are pretty well-known - e.g. performance of open-book models are better than parametric models, and Google search is better.\n\nWhy unreliable: \"the results are well-known\" should not be the reason for rejection. The experiments of this paper are simply to demonstrate the performance superiority of the internet-augmented method, instead of \"demonstrating any conclusion\".\n\n**Example 6**\n> Reviewer wrote: I would like to see the empirical results on stronger models, like RoBERTa related models.\n\nWhy unreliable: This is an example of a weak review provided by ACL It is not weak to have all the baselines not covered in the paper.\n\n**Example 7**\n> Reviewer wrote: More importantly, it requires highly-structured and large-scale in-domain data for pretraining, which might not be available in other domains.\n\nWhy unreliable: Perhaps there are other domains where there is a large amount of highly structured data. For example, data in the financial domain contains information about stock prices, stock reviews and other information.\n\n**Example 8**\n> Reviewer wrote: -- SentBERT \u2192 SentenceBERT or SBERT\n\nWhy unreliable: This is an overly harsh typo inspection.\n\n**Example 9**\n> Reviewer wrote: Various efficient routing functions should be compared with in this work, as intelligent routing functions achieve similar effects of improving parameter efficiency.\n\nWhy unreliable: Expert sharing and routing are orthogonal ideas\n\n**Example 10**\n> Reviewer wrote: The proposed method (objectives) is hard to transfer to other domains.\n\nWhy unreliable: Most pre-trained objectives that incorporate domain knowledge have such problems. However, it is still a meaningful research for a specific domain.\n\n### Superficial Review\n\n*The reviewer appears to have only skimmed the paper, providing generic or unsupported comments about the presence or absence of weaknesses.*\n\n**Example 1**\n> Reviewer wrote: The proposed continual knowledge learning problem is quite interesting and important.\n\nWhy unreliable: This statement is quite vague even though it is a strength. It does not provide enough context to other reviewers or the AC as to the paper's exact strengths.\n\n**Example 2**\n> Reviewer wrote: The FUAR metric is also technically sound.\n\nWhy unreliable: This statement is very vague and not reliable as there are no supporting evidence for the claim.\n\n**Example 3**\n> Reviewer wrote: Quality and Significance: Overall, this is a good application method and might be useful for many practical applications.\n\nWhy unreliable: Need more detail for this claim. There is very little context to the statement making it not very reliable.\n\n**Example 4**\n> Reviewer wrote: Compared to the FCM in this paper, I think the performance of causal masking methods from [2] might be better.\n\nWhy unreliable: This claim is not backed by evidence\n\n**Example 5**\n> Reviewer wrote: This paper proposes a new few-shot learning method for NLP problems by incorporating a simple,effective framework.\n\nWhy unreliable: The summary is too general and not specific to the main focus of this paper.\n\n**Example 6**\n> Reviewer wrote: Designing metrics for exposure bias is a novel task, and this paper invented appropriate approaches.\n\nWhy unreliable: This work is not demonstrated to be novel and the statement is too brief to explain the novelty of the work.\n\n**Example 7**\n> Reviewer wrote: The empirical results are solid and strong.\n\nWhy unreliable: The experiments are kind of limited in a narrow domain for the proposed general method. Thus, it is difficult to say results are solid and strong.\n\n**Example 8**\n> Reviewer wrote: The proposed continual knowledge learning problem is quite interesting and important. The benchmark is useful and the proposed FUAR metric is technically sound.\n\nWhy unreliable: Repetitive comments without any description of the comments made. The reviewer already mentions the same before and in both cases provides no supporting evidence for the claim.\n\n**Example 9**\n> Reviewer wrote: The paper doesn't have too many weaknesses.\n\nWhy unreliable: This statement is superficial and indicates that the reviewer might not spend enough time checking the details of this paper.\n\n**Example 10**\n> Reviewer wrote: The authors did mention one ablation in the supplementary that I missed, but I don\u2019t think that is sufficient for a reader to understand how to build on this method in this future without re-running all the experiments, doing an extensive hyperparam search, etc.\n\nWhy unreliable: This comment is too general. Most papers have this limitation.\n\n### Unstated statement\n\n*Statements made in the review are not supported by content in the paper.*\n\n**Example 1**\n> Reviewer wrote: Ease of use, being conceivably language agnostic, training more efficiently and allowing easy reimplementation (pytorch), make this work a welcome contribution to the field.\n\nWhy unreliable: This paper did not upload the code when it was submitted, and the conclusion of this review cannot be verified.\n\n**Example 2**\n> Reviewer wrote: Like previous work, they perform multitask prompted fine-tuning on 40+ NLP tasks but while retrieving from external knowledge sources to perform the task.\n\nWhy unreliable: There is no (multitask prompted) fine-tuning on 40+ NLP tasks in this paper\n\n### Excessive demands\n\n*if the weaknesses are just asking for excessive things that are not necessary for a good paper.*\n\n_No examples available in the source dataset._\n\n### Generic comment\n\n*weaknesses are just generic comments that can apply to any paper, without really pointing out the specific problems of the paper.*\n\n_No examples available in the source dataset._\n\n## Reliable (valid) weakness examples\n\nEach example below is a real reviewer weakness segment annotated by a human as RELIABLE (a genuine, well-grounded criticism). Use these to calibrate: many critical-sounding weaknesses ARE reliable. Only mark reliable=0 when the segment clearly matches one of the error patterns above.\n\n**Example 1**\n> Reviewer wrote: Lack of detail analysis on the computational efficiency.\n\n**Example 2**\n> Reviewer wrote: In order to improve the paper, I recommend providing more insights into the workings of the method and the bias that the fixed structure like tucker decomposition can lead to.\n\n**Example 3**\n> Reviewer wrote: It is unclear why such big memories are still improving the model.\n\n**Example 4**\n> Reviewer wrote: Sec 4. --If your introduced bitext retrieval objective uses batch size, experiments comparing the effect of batch size is necessary.\n\n**Example 5**\n> Reviewer wrote: Maybe a logical tree of some sort can help improve readability here.\n\n**Example 6**\n> Reviewer wrote: Another choice made by the authors is to use only the values in FIM corresponding to the adapters.\n\n**Example 7**\n> Reviewer wrote: I don't see the point of compare different variants of LLMs with or without pre-training.\n\n**Example 8**\n> Reviewer wrote: This is not critical: this is still acceptable for the first observation.\n\n**Example 9**\n> Reviewer wrote: However, there are some concerns about experimental settings.\n\n**Example 10**\n> Reviewer wrote: Besides, the data gathering method also relies on hand-designed templates (Line 220).\n\n**Example 11**\n> Reviewer wrote: I am unclear of some of the experimental settings and how much we can conclude from them.\n\n**Example 12**\n> Reviewer wrote: I know that this can make the size of compressed model really amazing (e.g., 1.8M) and the compression ratio amazing (e.g., 86M/12.3M=7) but is not fair as the whole model including the embedding layer are used when deploying.\n\n**Example 13**\n> Reviewer wrote: There are some hints that the gains might be partly related to regularization (eg, better results on IWSLT than WMT).\n\n**Example 14**\n> Reviewer wrote: The meaning of the different-colored tokens could also be explained in the caption.\n\n**Example 15**\n> Reviewer wrote: The proposed method is novel in that it is effective and scalable.\n\n**Example 16**\n> Reviewer wrote: To explain more previous weakness, I was not clear when reading if it is enough to do prompt re-formatting or aggregation is also necessary.\n\n**Example 17**\n> Reviewer wrote: This is mentioned several times in the manuscript but it looks to just boil down to using the aforementioned prompting technique.\n\n**Example 18**\n> Reviewer wrote: I don't think the paper, overall, is particularly novel.\n\n**Example 19**\n> Reviewer wrote: (2) Unclear cost for running the proposed methods against the standard-prompting models.\n\n**Example 20**\n> Reviewer wrote: However, it would have been interesting to see tasks or domains where improvement is more significant.\n\n**Example 21**\n> Reviewer wrote: To add value, authors should have explored other text summarization datasets with different writing styles to really bring out the different style biases rather than just paraphrasing.\n"
const TASKS = [{"seg_id": 0, "paper_idx": 0, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_0.txt", "segment_text": "For the AMA prompts on Appendix H, it seems that some tasks (DROP, SST, BoolQ, COPA) only do either prompt() or answer() stage, not both. How can we decide \"automatically\" when to do only prompt() or answer() or both depending on the target task?"}, {"seg_id": 3, "paper_idx": 1, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_1.txt", "segment_text": "The novelty is primarily empirical, and the technical approach is essentially an application of known techniques (the standard pretrain-finetune paradigm of LLMs)."}, {"seg_id": 4, "paper_idx": 1, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_1.txt", "segment_text": "Novelty: The novelty is primarily empirical, and the technical approach is essentially an application of known techniques (the standard pretrain-finetune paradigm of LLMs)."}, {"seg_id": 5, "paper_idx": 1, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_1.txt", "segment_text": "However, the lack of technical novelty & contribution makes this work slightly below the acceptance bar of the conference."}, {"seg_id": 6, "paper_idx": 1, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_1.txt", "segment_text": "The modeling part lacks novelty, just basic pre-training + fine-tuning on LLMs, and seems there's no model design based on the features of HTML?"}, {"seg_id": 7, "paper_idx": 1, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_1.txt", "segment_text": "My main concern is that the new task is not clearly justified, and the modeling part might be too standard -- there is no HTML-specific model design involved, just studying basic LLMs."}, {"seg_id": 9, "paper_idx": 1, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_1.txt", "segment_text": "I examined the supplementary material provided by the authors and only found the data files."}, {"seg_id": 10, "paper_idx": 1, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_1.txt", "segment_text": "Either code or more detailed experimental setup should be provided in the supplementary material"}, {"seg_id": 11, "paper_idx": 1, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_1.txt", "segment_text": "It is better to make the codes available for reproducing these results."}, {"seg_id": 13, "paper_idx": 1, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_1.txt", "segment_text": "I expect to see more task-specific models such as StructuralLM [1], MarkupLM [2] that respect the tree structure of HTML (or some other methods mentioned in the paper) to be evaluated against."}, {"seg_id": 14, "paper_idx": 1, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_1.txt", "segment_text": "Basically, I need to see the following question answered: \"For specific web tasks, do I want to use a HTML specific model trained on the small dataset or just to fine-tune LLMs\"?"}, {"seg_id": 15, "paper_idx": 1, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_1.txt", "segment_text": "On MiniWoB, it's not very surprising that using a larger model with pre-training, you can beat smaller models from scratch."}, {"seg_id": 17, "paper_idx": 2, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_2.txt", "segment_text": "As mentioned in the paper, NLI models do not transfer well to out-of-domain NLI samples, not to mention non-NLI tasks."}, {"seg_id": 22, "paper_idx": 3, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_3.txt", "segment_text": "There is a lack of technical details. In particular, it is not exactly clear how the sum step is performed. If this follows a probabilistic framework, please write down the steps formally."}, {"seg_id": 23, "paper_idx": 3, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_3.txt", "segment_text": "Without clear writing, it is hard to evaluate novelty."}, {"seg_id": 24, "paper_idx": 3, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_3.txt", "segment_text": "The writing is not clear, making it hard to evaluate the contribution of this paper."}, {"seg_id": 28, "paper_idx": 3, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_3.txt", "segment_text": "This is doable for 5 choices, but not so much for 100 choices or more (in SQuAD-type QA, the output could be any combinations of tokens) which is extremely expensive."}, {"seg_id": 31, "paper_idx": 6, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_6.txt", "segment_text": "The proposed model follows the framework of generative retrieval models but applies contextualized token embeddings."}, {"seg_id": 34, "paper_idx": 6, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_6.txt", "segment_text": "GENRE uses BART-large whereas this paper uses T5, so the focus on comparing directly with GENRE seems a bit misguided."}, {"seg_id": 35, "paper_idx": 6, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_6.txt", "segment_text": "The main KILT results only have the original GENRE (using a different pre-trained model, BART) and CGR (even though the authors listed DPR and SEAL, the numbers are not in the same comparable settings)."}, {"seg_id": 37, "paper_idx": 6, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_6.txt", "segment_text": "I can guess what it does, but it's never fully clear."}, {"seg_id": 38, "paper_idx": 6, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_6.txt", "segment_text": "The novelty contribution is somehow limited."}, {"seg_id": 42, "paper_idx": 6, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_6.txt", "segment_text": "The proposed method makes sense but the contribution of adding a contextualized embedding space might be considered as light."}, {"seg_id": 44, "paper_idx": 7, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_7.txt", "segment_text": "The tasks may be too simple / short / toy."}, {"seg_id": 45, "paper_idx": 7, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_7.txt", "segment_text": "It is quite hard to draw many conclusions about the best performing systems because their training strategies are not public, and it is not clear what types of task ambiguity have been seen during training."}, {"seg_id": 46, "paper_idx": 7, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_7.txt", "segment_text": "However, the paper relies heavily on opaque models, and it is a little hard to draw meaningful conclusions about the best performing systems."}, {"seg_id": 47, "paper_idx": 7, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_7.txt", "segment_text": "Besides, the empirical observation and proposed fine-tuning method are pretty straightforward and not surprising."}, {"seg_id": 48, "paper_idx": 7, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_7.txt", "segment_text": "The observation that the best language model achieve similar performance to human on such ambiguous task is not surprising, even though the setting is synthetic and simple."}, {"seg_id": 50, "paper_idx": 7, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_7.txt", "segment_text": "However, for the same reason, I am less convinced about the generality of findings reported here."}, {"seg_id": 51, "paper_idx": 7, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_7.txt", "segment_text": "This paper only studies the ambiguity of a few toy tasks, which only require simple lexical understanding and the texts are synthetic and very short."}, {"seg_id": 52, "paper_idx": 7, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_7.txt", "segment_text": "It is not clear how these findings would extend to more complex task types."}, {"seg_id": 56, "paper_idx": 7, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_7.txt", "segment_text": "What are some other types of task ambiguities in NLP tasks, maybe more subtle than simply occluding a category name?"}, {"seg_id": 57, "paper_idx": 7, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_7.txt", "segment_text": "However, the current study focuses on a very narrow type of ambiguity, and it's not clear how much these insights will extend to other types of task ambiguity."}, {"seg_id": 61, "paper_idx": 9, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_9.txt", "segment_text": "While used components are effective, each of them is from other works."}, {"seg_id": 62, "paper_idx": 9, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_9.txt", "segment_text": "In other words, UniDoc is a combination of well-known techniques, having a lack of significant novelty."}, {"seg_id": 63, "paper_idx": 9, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_9.txt", "segment_text": "Although they do make sense and are reasonable choices for a multimodal framework, I see little novelty here."}, {"seg_id": 64, "paper_idx": 9, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_9.txt", "segment_text": "Originality: Apart from some novelties of the pretraining objectives, the method reuses some of the existing architectures in a hierarchical way."}, {"seg_id": 65, "paper_idx": 9, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_9.txt", "segment_text": "UniDoc does not improve the performance of document classification tasks over previous methods."}, {"seg_id": 66, "paper_idx": 9, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_9.txt", "segment_text": "Although the authors argue that it attributes to the difference of OCR engines, they should explain the magnitude of this difference."}, {"seg_id": 67, "paper_idx": 9, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_9.txt", "segment_text": "They say the difference between EasyOCR and Tesseract, but should report performance metric and qualitative analysis on the RVL-CDIP dataset to compare real Microsoft OCR and EasyOCR."}, {"seg_id": 68, "paper_idx": 9, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_9.txt", "segment_text": "I feel that the methods/model architectures are largely putting together existing pieces of work from vision and language fields."}, {"seg_id": 69, "paper_idx": 9, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_9.txt", "segment_text": "In Sec 3.1, the proposed feature extraction, quantization, and gated cross-attention modules are all rather straightforward variations of existing work (i.e., the essential mechanisms are not new)."}, {"seg_id": 70, "paper_idx": 9, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_9.txt", "segment_text": "The methods are not novel/insightful as they are largely borrowing from existing work."}, {"seg_id": 82, "paper_idx": 11, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_11.txt", "segment_text": "Yet the proposed method is somewhat trivial since it only uses an ensemble of classifiers and uses the cosine similarity to calculate the final similarity score calculation."}, {"seg_id": 86, "paper_idx": 11, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_11.txt", "segment_text": "SPE can be considered as a sentence similarity metric."}, {"seg_id": 93, "paper_idx": 12, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_12.txt", "segment_text": "\u2018We select 5 tasks where teacher can most significantly improve\u2026\u2019 - I don\u2019t understand the motivation behind these choices."}, {"seg_id": 99, "paper_idx": 13, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_13.txt", "segment_text": "I think the analysis of model size is quite helpful, since a natural question is whether or not these results are due to the ELECTRA small model being too small to benefit from pre-training on massive upstream datasets."}, {"seg_id": 103, "paper_idx": 13, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_13.txt", "segment_text": "The discussion on how to construct artificial long-term dependencies is very interesting, but it also is a little bit orthogonal to the remainder of the paper; at least with the current framing."}, {"seg_id": 104, "paper_idx": 13, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_13.txt", "segment_text": "Despite the very impressive results, I think the elephant in the room, which I was surprised not to see any mention of in the paper, is how much do the results have to to do with the corpus being one of the downstream task corpus, or simply a smaller corpus."}, {"seg_id": 107, "paper_idx": 13, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_13.txt", "segment_text": "It seems possible that the models can acquire linguistic and factual knowledge from these datasets during pre-training, which cannot be learnt efficiently from only predicting the downstream task labels."}, {"seg_id": 108, "paper_idx": 13, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_13.txt", "segment_text": "That is, a baseline of pretraining on a general pretrain corpus of similar size to the downstream corpora is missing and is very important to shed light on the results here."}, {"seg_id": 110, "paper_idx": 13, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_13.txt", "segment_text": "I do think the results are somewhat surprising and novel, although there's a bit of overclaiming (since I suspect that these results are largely due to the similarity of the downstream datasets)."}, {"seg_id": 115, "paper_idx": 14, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_14.txt", "segment_text": "Although the usage of documentation for code generation is novel, it is somewhat simple from a technical point of view."}, {"seg_id": 117, "paper_idx": 14, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_14.txt", "segment_text": "However, in terms of technical novelty, already existing models and techniques are used for retrieval and generation."}, {"seg_id": 118, "paper_idx": 14, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_14.txt", "segment_text": "Quality: Well-designed and experiments and well-written work."}, {"seg_id": 119, "paper_idx": 14, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_14.txt", "segment_text": "The idea of retrieving documentation for prompting seems interesting and novel to me."}, {"seg_id": 122, "paper_idx": 15, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_15.txt", "segment_text": "Second, the baselines used for comparison are not complete, which makes me concern about the effectiveness of the model."}, {"seg_id": 129, "paper_idx": 15, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_15.txt", "segment_text": "There are many settings considered in the paper and it is unclear if the proposed contributions are truly significant due to weak baselines are differences in data used for training different methods."}, {"seg_id": 130, "paper_idx": 15, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_15.txt", "segment_text": "Ideally, the model should be compared against a skip-thought baseline or an unsupervised sentence representation learning method that uses the same resources for training."}, {"seg_id": 132, "paper_idx": 15, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_15.txt", "segment_text": "I would appreciate a better and fair comparison of the Skip-Thought model by using same transformer encoder and same training corpus."}, {"seg_id": 136, "paper_idx": 16, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_16.txt", "segment_text": "Of course, there's nothing wrong with this, but it breaks a crucial assumptions about how the NLI data (in particular the SNLI data) was sourced: the events and entities in the hypothesis refer to the events and entities in the premise as much as possible."}, {"seg_id": 137, "paper_idx": 16, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_16.txt", "segment_text": "1. The data contribution seems small and somewhat unnatural. The hypothesis format seems extremely unnatural (being text referential). I wonder if such examples are out of domain for the trained NLI systems."}, {"seg_id": 139, "paper_idx": 16, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_16.txt", "segment_text": "It is not clear why the neutral label is removed and the problem is converted into a binary problem of deciding whether the hypothesis is entailed or contradicted."}, {"seg_id": 140, "paper_idx": 16, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_16.txt", "segment_text": "It seems that most of the hypotheses would actually be neutral, and a good model should allocate most of its probability mass to the neutral label."}, {"seg_id": 141, "paper_idx": 16, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_16.txt", "segment_text": "2.The bias measurement forces the models to predict either entailment or contradiction, where in fact the ground truth answer, in my opinion, for the proposed NLI examples, is neutral."}, {"seg_id": 142, "paper_idx": 16, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_16.txt", "segment_text": "For all we know, the models are correctly predicting that with high probability, but the measurement is forcing a renormalization between entailment and contradiction examples (Section 3.2)."}, {"seg_id": 150, "paper_idx": 16, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_16.txt", "segment_text": "For instance, replace \"he\" or \"his\" in the existing hypothesis with \"she\" or \"her\" so you can have female hypothesis and male hypothesis."}, {"seg_id": 151, "paper_idx": 16, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_16.txt", "segment_text": "When analyzing the results, the authors disregard the neutral case."}, {"seg_id": 152, "paper_idx": 16, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_16.txt", "segment_text": "Why do we have to force a choice between entail and contradict, when a stereotype-free model would actually predict neutral?"}, {"seg_id": 166, "paper_idx": 19, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_19.txt", "segment_text": "And the reason why LSTM is more robust is that the model size is very small such that it can relearn all weights."}, {"seg_id": 167, "paper_idx": 19, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_19.txt", "segment_text": "My concern is that these scrambling techniques might push your dataset outside of real word adversarial attacks."}, {"seg_id": 174, "paper_idx": 20, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_20.txt", "segment_text": "If it is sensitive to noise, then why it renders descent results on the other three datasets?"}, {"seg_id": 177, "paper_idx": 20, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_20.txt", "segment_text": "The new data augmentation, while resulting in performance improvements over kFolden and Contrastive, also has more dependencies, i.e., access to GPT3."}, {"seg_id": 180, "paper_idx": 20, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_20.txt", "segment_text": "What happens if one generates novel labels with smaller models?"}, {"seg_id": 181, "paper_idx": 20, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_20.txt", "segment_text": "The new loss function outperforms OE mostly because OE seems to fail on TREC-10 (else, it's competitive)."}, {"seg_id": 182, "paper_idx": 20, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_20.txt", "segment_text": "(3) Looking at Table 1, I am curious why OE+novelty prompting performs such badly on TREC-10."}, {"seg_id": 184, "paper_idx": 20, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_20.txt", "segment_text": "The biggest negative of the paper, in my view, is that the performance gains (particularly of the new loss function) are marginal."}, {"seg_id": 186, "paper_idx": 20, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_20.txt", "segment_text": "The authors method can only work with text classification tasks, which is great, but --- some of the competing methods are more general, and the Wikitext + CCL method (which simulates the more general setting of not having GPT-3 but only an OOD set) only marginally outperforms prior solutions (kFolden, for example, doesn't even depend on the external set)."}, {"seg_id": 188, "paper_idx": 22, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_22.txt", "segment_text": "However, as shown in Table 2, the proposed method is not better than SpanBERT."}, {"seg_id": 192, "paper_idx": 22, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_22.txt", "segment_text": "Since the improvement of the proposed method is marginal (~ 1% avg) and fine-tuning BERT on GLUE tasks might have high variance, authors should report the mean and std over multiple runs."}, {"seg_id": 193, "paper_idx": 22, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_22.txt", "segment_text": "Employing span-level information in PrLMs was proposed in previous works such as SpanBERT."}, {"seg_id": 197, "paper_idx": 25, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_25.txt", "segment_text": "Lack of statistical tests to evaluate significance."}, {"seg_id": 198, "paper_idx": 25, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_25.txt", "segment_text": "In some cases improvements are quite small."}, {"seg_id": 206, "paper_idx": 26, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_26.txt", "segment_text": "The hierarchical relationship labels are not convincing enough. It is even difficult for humans to predict whether two sentences are from the same document. Enforcing the model to learn the \"same document\" label might cause the overfitting of the sentence representations."}, {"seg_id": 213, "paper_idx": 27, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_27.txt", "segment_text": "While the results are interesting and illustrate the flexibility of a standard transformer language model, the key ingredient of this approach relies on having a language model that has seen a very large and diverse dataset that very likely includes some translation examples (for zero-shot translation to work)."}, {"seg_id": 220, "paper_idx": 27, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_27.txt", "segment_text": "Only one non-English language is considered."}, {"seg_id": 221, "paper_idx": 27, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_27.txt", "segment_text": "The choice of language pair also hinders this study."}, {"seg_id": 223, "paper_idx": 27, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_27.txt", "segment_text": "This paper merely verifies the effectiveness of the proposed method on the English-French language pair, making the experiment results not convincing enough."}, {"seg_id": 230, "paper_idx": 27, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_27.txt", "segment_text": "Or Gujarati < - - > English?"}, {"seg_id": 232, "paper_idx": 27, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_27.txt", "segment_text": "I am looking forward to observing the unsupervised translation performance of the proposed method on some distant language pairs."}, {"seg_id": 233, "paper_idx": 28, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_28.txt", "segment_text": "The novelty of this paper is limited."}, {"seg_id": 236, "paper_idx": 28, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_28.txt", "segment_text": "No novel/brand new ideas."}, {"seg_id": 237, "paper_idx": 28, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_28.txt", "segment_text": "The submission is an analysis paper rather than finding something new."}, {"seg_id": 240, "paper_idx": 30, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_30.txt", "segment_text": "However, the technical contribution of the paper is quite limited."}, {"seg_id": 241, "paper_idx": 30, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_30.txt", "segment_text": "From model perspective, there seems be have few innovation."}, {"seg_id": 243, "paper_idx": 31, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_31.txt", "segment_text": "The authors name a lot of related works, but compare only very few of them in the experiments."}, {"seg_id": 244, "paper_idx": 31, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_31.txt", "segment_text": "Though the introduced decomposition methods are not new, the adaption to the Transformer layers and corresponding analysis are comprehensive."}, {"seg_id": 254, "paper_idx": 31, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_31.txt", "segment_text": "I can understand the similarity of the attention query weight vectors across the layers, but I can\u2019t understand why all the weights are merged."}, {"seg_id": 258, "paper_idx": 33, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_33.txt", "segment_text": "Inference Batch, batch size and its importance: The batch size at inference seems like an important variable."}, {"seg_id": 260, "paper_idx": 33, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_33.txt", "segment_text": "The grammar of the paper will benefit from having a native english speaker proof-read it."}, {"seg_id": 261, "paper_idx": 33, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_33.txt", "segment_text": "E.g., \"as well as remaining the pretrained models unmodified\" can be re-written as \"without modifying the pretrained model parameters\"."}, {"seg_id": 263, "paper_idx": 34, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_34.txt", "segment_text": "The casual masking method proposed by this paper is quite similar to [2] and HYBUNI in [1]. [2] also adds some masks on the basis of casual language modeling."}, {"seg_id": 264, "paper_idx": 35, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_35.txt", "segment_text": "The actual method is simple combination of existing ideas."}, {"seg_id": 265, "paper_idx": 35, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_35.txt", "segment_text": "Also given this paper unifies existing approaches and adapts them for prompt tuning setup, it is marginally significant/ novel."}, {"seg_id": 266, "paper_idx": 35, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_35.txt", "segment_text": "By definition, the lifelong learning paradigm deals with a large number of tasks in sequence."}, {"seg_id": 267, "paper_idx": 35, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_35.txt", "segment_text": "However, the paper evaluates the proposed approach on the sequence of 2-4 tasks."}, {"seg_id": 268, "paper_idx": 35, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_35.txt", "segment_text": "So it is unclear whether the gains persist as we increase the number of tasks to 10-15 (towards a more realistic evaluation setup)."}, {"seg_id": 270, "paper_idx": 35, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_35.txt", "segment_text": "E.g., it would be great to show more than 3 tasks."}, {"seg_id": 272, "paper_idx": 35, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_35.txt", "segment_text": "The number of tasks and domains is minimal setting."}, {"seg_id": 274, "paper_idx": 37, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_37.txt", "segment_text": "However, after this introduction and motivation, it reads more like a typical optimizer paper introducing a new optimizer based on hand-wavy intuitions."}, {"seg_id": 277, "paper_idx": 37, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_37.txt", "segment_text": "For an \"optimizer\" paper, it is weak: hand-wavy motivation, no real derivation of update rule or even convergence proofs, and experiments only in one very specific domain: transfer of pre-trained transformer models."}, {"seg_id": 279, "paper_idx": 37, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_37.txt", "segment_text": "Architecture: The method is evaluated only with transformer architectures."}, {"seg_id": 280, "paper_idx": 37, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_37.txt", "segment_text": "7) All experimental evaluation is about fine-tuning of already pre-trained transformer models."}, {"seg_id": 281, "paper_idx": 37, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_37.txt", "segment_text": "It would be interesting to have results on ImageNet only where the SAGE method is used during the training."}, {"seg_id": 284, "paper_idx": 38, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_38.txt", "segment_text": "Alternatively, a human evaluation experiment using the synthetic data could provide more evidence that the bias is really introduced by the summarization models."}, {"seg_id": 291, "paper_idx": 38, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_38.txt", "segment_text": "Lack of some human validation"}, {"seg_id": 292, "paper_idx": 38, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_38.txt", "segment_text": "Some examples: Figure 7 has no legend, Table 3's caption says some text is in red and other in blue, but the only color there is yellow."}, {"seg_id": 294, "paper_idx": 38, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_38.txt", "segment_text": "In the paraphrasing of articles, the authors do not show how good the paraphrased sentences are."}, {"seg_id": 295, "paper_idx": 38, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_38.txt", "segment_text": "A human evaluation of a few paraphrased sentences would have cleared this issue."}, {"seg_id": 298, "paper_idx": 38, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_38.txt", "segment_text": "Summarization is not an objective task, so the gold standard is to show resulting summaries to humans and see whether they prefer one model or the other."}, {"seg_id": 314, "paper_idx": 41, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_41.txt", "segment_text": "(Mixed) Charformer achieves worse predictive performance than subword models BERT and T5, which seems to suggest the proposed GBST gives little lift from character-level to subword-level."}, {"seg_id": 315, "paper_idx": 41, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_41.txt", "segment_text": "Even though some of the results are competitive, the experiments do not provide enough evidence that the learned tokenization is crucial for achieving the results."}, {"seg_id": 316, "paper_idx": 41, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_41.txt", "segment_text": "In addition, there is no proper evaluation of the learned segmentations other than the visualization of the learned weights for a single example."}, {"seg_id": 318, "paper_idx": 41, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_41.txt", "segment_text": "Also, one of the claimed benefits of the proposed method is that is faster is mainly due to the fixed downsampling which is not something new and applies to other character-level baselines."}, {"seg_id": 322, "paper_idx": 41, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_41.txt", "segment_text": "In this paper, the scaled Charformer-SBase is also much better than the unscaled Charformer-Base."}, {"seg_id": 333, "paper_idx": 41, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_41.txt", "segment_text": "similar vocabulary size and contextualizer design), but this was not explored at all in the evaluation."}, {"seg_id": 337, "paper_idx": 41, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_41.txt", "segment_text": "Lack of experiment on different sizes of the model, especially performance when with a larger scale."}, {"seg_id": 338, "paper_idx": 41, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_41.txt", "segment_text": "All the experiment focusing on models (other than mT5) with a parameter scale of 200M."}, {"seg_id": 339, "paper_idx": 41, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_41.txt", "segment_text": "Since this model saves model size, would it achieve better performance compared with the same size (e.g., 200M like Byte-level T5 Base in Table 1)?"}, {"seg_id": 340, "paper_idx": 41, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_41.txt", "segment_text": "But the paper ByT5\u2019s smallest model has 300M parameters and performs much better than with 200M parameters (comparing Table 4 of ByT5 and Table 4 of this paper)."}, {"seg_id": 341, "paper_idx": 42, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_42.txt", "segment_text": "3. (minor) The \u2018zero-shot\u2019 experiments really seem to be \u2018few-shot\u2019 tests since you provide the model with 8 or so examples."}, {"seg_id": 342, "paper_idx": 42, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_42.txt", "segment_text": "addition of entity information) seems under-explored."}, {"seg_id": 343, "paper_idx": 42, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_42.txt", "segment_text": "Since the main point of the paper is adding entity information to the models, the paper would be better to include more explanations about the entity information."}, {"seg_id": 345, "paper_idx": 42, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_42.txt", "segment_text": "Large KALM has close to 700M params and not 300M as indicated."}, {"seg_id": 347, "paper_idx": 42, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_42.txt", "segment_text": "Need extra parameters to save entity embedding (~471M)."}, {"seg_id": 348, "paper_idx": 42, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_42.txt", "segment_text": "However, as the authors mentioned, this can be viewed as an external memory module and embedding lookup doesn't need much computation."}, {"seg_id": 350, "paper_idx": 44, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_44.txt", "segment_text": "The method is only broadly applied to the 540B model that almost no one else has access to/can train or fine-tune."}, {"seg_id": 351, "paper_idx": 44, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_44.txt", "segment_text": "This claim is very not supported by the experiments where the results are shown for just one LM and that too is not openly available."}, {"seg_id": 352, "paper_idx": 44, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_44.txt", "segment_text": "The reproducibility of the results is a concern to many."}, {"seg_id": 353, "paper_idx": 44, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_44.txt", "segment_text": "Replicating this is nearly impossible."}, {"seg_id": 355, "paper_idx": 44, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_44.txt", "segment_text": "However, the results cannot be reproduced due to the lack of open source policy for PaLM by google and there is no way to test if the results are accurate or not."}, {"seg_id": 356, "paper_idx": 44, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_44.txt", "segment_text": "The method has not been tested on models that are of reasonable size or ones that are publicly available, which is a significant limitation."}, {"seg_id": 357, "paper_idx": 44, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_44.txt", "segment_text": "Finally the limitation of the experiments on one model (and that too not open sourced) is not enough to justify the big claim of \"large language models can self improve\"."}, {"seg_id": 359, "paper_idx": 44, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_44.txt", "segment_text": "Secondly, proposing an approach and claiming that \u201cThis is similar to how a human brain sometimes learns: given a question, think multiple times to derive different possible results, conclude on how the question should be solved, and then learn from or memorize its own solution\u201d is just random with not theory or study to back this up."}, {"seg_id": 363, "paper_idx": 46, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_46.txt", "segment_text": "If each word in NLP sequences is a span, the proposed method is the same as the word-level dropout baseline studied in previous works, e.g., [1]."}, {"seg_id": 366, "paper_idx": 46, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_46.txt", "segment_text": "Limited novelty."}, {"seg_id": 367, "paper_idx": 46, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_46.txt", "segment_text": "(1) limited novelty."}, {"seg_id": 368, "paper_idx": 46, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_46.txt", "segment_text": "My major concern is the lack of comparison of other baselines."}, {"seg_id": 369, "paper_idx": 46, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_46.txt", "segment_text": "As reviewed in the second paragraph, there are many Transformer variants proposed for long sequences, but none of them is compared."}, {"seg_id": 371, "paper_idx": 46, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_46.txt", "segment_text": "In section5, the author discuss a bunch of related works on data augmentation, however, in natural language experiments these are not compared."}, {"seg_id": 379, "paper_idx": 47, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_47.txt", "segment_text": "The authors could include an appendix or discussion on how many of the tests could be translated successfully and what incorrect translations look like - e.g. tests that perform a sequence of APIs or expect exceptions."}, {"seg_id": 381, "paper_idx": 47, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_47.txt", "segment_text": "Method to generate test cases for translated Python and C++ codes seems to be dependent on expected input/output pairs, and it's not clear how the proposed method would work for more complicated code segments, or for languages with no direct mapping of data types and constructs."}, {"seg_id": 382, "paper_idx": 47, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_47.txt", "segment_text": "While I agree that unit tests tend to be inputs and outputs for which translation should be easier, this is probably limiting the scope of what can possibly be translated."}, {"seg_id": 383, "paper_idx": 47, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_47.txt", "segment_text": "Instantiating such variables may rely on imported classes or require translating extensive surrounding code context other than the unit test itself."}, {"seg_id": 384, "paper_idx": 47, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_47.txt", "segment_text": "Authors claim that the unit tests suites can be used to test program semantics in any programming languages, provided there is a way to establish the mapping between types and parameters."}, {"seg_id": 385, "paper_idx": 47, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_47.txt", "segment_text": "Or, how would this procedure work when there isn't a direct mapping of data types and constructs between the languages."}, {"seg_id": 390, "paper_idx": 47, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_47.txt", "segment_text": "Quality of generated tests dips on more complicated code segments, and we do not know what effect that has on the performance."}, {"seg_id": 404, "paper_idx": 49, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_49.txt", "segment_text": "My biggest complaint is that it's not completely clear the instructions themselves are important at all - I suggest a few more experiments, though they don't seem crucial."}, {"seg_id": 405, "paper_idx": 50, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_50.txt", "segment_text": "the trace of a Hessian matrix in the formulation from this paper is very easy to obtain and one doesn't even need to materialise the matrix. The issue is that the Hessian matrix is evaluated around the global minimum of a model being trained on the pre-training data, and, without any knowledge of the pre-training data, it becomes very difficult to compute the Hessian matrix."}, {"seg_id": 406, "paper_idx": 50, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_50.txt", "segment_text": "The claim that flatness can decide the downstream performance is not well-supported."}, {"seg_id": 408, "paper_idx": 50, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_50.txt", "segment_text": "It is not clear how the size of models and flat minima is related to each other."}, {"seg_id": 409, "paper_idx": 50, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_50.txt", "segment_text": "One possible explanation is that the flatness could be a consequence of scaling up the model size, and the good performance is brought by the large model size, too."}, {"seg_id": 417, "paper_idx": 51, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_51.txt", "segment_text": "Given this challenge, I am not entirely sure whether this GPT-2 fine-tuning or GPT3 generation is very novel for this paper to be accepted as ICLR paper."}, {"seg_id": 418, "paper_idx": 51, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_51.txt", "segment_text": "Considering a GPT-2 is incorporated additional for data generation, it is not surprising to observe improvements."}, {"seg_id": 419, "paper_idx": 51, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_51.txt", "segment_text": "This can only show that using additional LM for data augmentation is useful and such a claim is provided in many existing papers."}, {"seg_id": 437, "paper_idx": 54, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_54.txt", "segment_text": "Can you provide a formal definition of a concept?"}, {"seg_id": 439, "paper_idx": 54, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_54.txt", "segment_text": "The number of programs is low (only 8), which the authors justify as balancing between obtaining robust results and limiting the human effort needed."}, {"seg_id": 440, "paper_idx": 54, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_54.txt", "segment_text": "Furthermore, even if we were satisfied with just accuracy, why not get the accuracy on a much larger sample size than 15 per concept (at least for the non-human learners)?"}, {"seg_id": 444, "paper_idx": 54, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_54.txt", "segment_text": "It is very possible that some of the empirical findings are specific to quirks in these 8 concepts."}, {"seg_id": 445, "paper_idx": 54, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_54.txt", "segment_text": "I would suggest keeping the current setup for the human comparisons, but greatly expanding the number of programs for the automated systems (much more than in the supplementary material)."}, {"seg_id": 447, "paper_idx": 54, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_54.txt", "segment_text": "I would say upfront that I am not an expert in this topic, and have found many points in the paper unclear, perhaps due to this."}, {"seg_id": 448, "paper_idx": 54, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_54.txt", "segment_text": "Methodologically, I'm a little concerned that the experiments don't fully address what the authors are after."}, {"seg_id": 450, "paper_idx": 54, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_54.txt", "segment_text": "In particular, it seems like what we'd really like to know is what concepts the learner has learned, but instead we measure accuracy."}, {"seg_id": 452, "paper_idx": 55, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_55.txt", "segment_text": "In particular, the paper uses \"fairness\" without clearly explaining how fairness is measured."}, {"seg_id": 454, "paper_idx": 55, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_55.txt", "segment_text": "However, it is hard to understand the experiment sections without referring to the appendix in detail."}, {"seg_id": 455, "paper_idx": 55, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_55.txt", "segment_text": "For example, in Table 2, the authors do not provide any description of the \"Metric/Method\" column in the main text."}, {"seg_id": 457, "paper_idx": 55, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_55.txt", "segment_text": "I would clearly describe in the caption of each figure what the rows/columns represent and what metric is used."}, {"seg_id": 461, "paper_idx": 55, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_55.txt", "segment_text": "A better word choice might be something like \"coverage\"."}, {"seg_id": 465, "paper_idx": 56, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_56.txt", "segment_text": "CodeGen model (single-turn) is only evaluated on a single dataset with only 164 problems. Why is the model not evaluated on other publicly available coding datasets such as MBPP and APPS?"}, {"seg_id": 466, "paper_idx": 56, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_56.txt", "segment_text": "Novelty is limited since there are already several pre-training/dataset collection papers in this area."}, {"seg_id": 472, "paper_idx": 57, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_57.txt", "segment_text": "The formulation doesn't scale to general reasoning problems, the tasks considered aren't tasks that you would train a specific LM to solve, and the algorithmic solution is very simple/trivial extension."}, {"seg_id": 474, "paper_idx": 57, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_57.txt", "segment_text": "On the negative side, the solution proposed is formulated as a fully supervised problem, meaning for each task the exact steps needed to solve the problem needs to be annotated."}, {"seg_id": 481, "paper_idx": 57, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_57.txt", "segment_text": "Therefore, it would be great if the authors could explain the limitation of the problems that RoT could solve and how to design proper subproblems in order to use RoT to train the models."}, {"seg_id": 483, "paper_idx": 57, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_57.txt", "segment_text": "The problems considered here are very simple, and an algorithm to solve them needs to be known in order to generate the training data."}, {"seg_id": 484, "paper_idx": 57, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_57.txt", "segment_text": "My main concern is on how to properly train the RoT framework as the training of RoT requires (non-trivial) human inputs to design proper subproblems."}, {"seg_id": 485, "paper_idx": 57, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_57.txt", "segment_text": "After decomposing the problem manually (i.e. by writing an algorithm to generate training data), the learning task is pretty simple (for addition, for example, all the model has to do is to extract the last digit from two numbers)."}, {"seg_id": 486, "paper_idx": 57, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_57.txt", "segment_text": "[-] The training of RoT requires (non-trivial) human inputs to design proper subproblems."}, {"seg_id": 487, "paper_idx": 57, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_57.txt", "segment_text": "My main concern is on how to properly train the RoT framework as it requires people to feed it with hand-designed subproblems."}, {"seg_id": 498, "paper_idx": 59, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_59.txt", "segment_text": "Many of the results presented in motivating the work are over random subsets of pre-training tasks and random seeds for training. However, none of the results seem to give an idea of the variance of performance, nor any indication of how many different random subsets of tasks were used for the results."}, {"seg_id": 503, "paper_idx": 59, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_59.txt", "segment_text": "Additionally, the models use the same learning rate regardless of batch-size, ostensibly a learning rate that was tuned for a batch-size of 512."}, {"seg_id": 504, "paper_idx": 59, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_59.txt", "segment_text": "Section 2.5 uses the same learning rate for different batch sizes, which might result in unfair comparison given there are previous works suggesting how batch size, learning rate could have compound effects on the downstream tasks."}, {"seg_id": 505, "paper_idx": 59, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_59.txt", "segment_text": "Section 2.5 compares different numbers of tasks trained on different batch-sizes."}, {"seg_id": 517, "paper_idx": 60, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_60.txt", "segment_text": "Table 2, if my understanding is correct, with knowledge case is presented after the vertical bar (|)."}, {"seg_id": 518, "paper_idx": 60, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_60.txt", "segment_text": "If that's true, please fix the description of the model which states the opposite."}, {"seg_id": 520, "paper_idx": 60, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_60.txt", "segment_text": "Some experimental setup details are missing, making the results difficult to be reproduced for now."}, {"seg_id": 523, "paper_idx": 60, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_60.txt", "segment_text": "While the motivation for identifying `hard cases' is 'limited bandwidth or budget to retrieve external knowledge', efficiency and computation time is not evaluated."}, {"seg_id": 524, "paper_idx": 60, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_60.txt", "segment_text": "The authors claim that they try to simulate the real-world case where they have limited bandwidth to retrieve external knowledge from the perspective of cost-efficiency, but the claim is not persuasive because the way they choose the portion of the queries to use external knowledge is through relative comparison between queries after scoring all other queries, and not through absolute scoring on each independent query, e.g., by applying a score threshold."}, {"seg_id": 534, "paper_idx": 62, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_62.txt", "segment_text": "However, we still rely on a vast amount of supervised data. In a way, the comparison with GPT-3 is not too fair."}, {"seg_id": 535, "paper_idx": 62, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_62.txt", "segment_text": "Questions Can authors provide intuition about the performance of T0 vs FLAN on the datasets that T0 does better, or worse?"}, {"seg_id": 536, "paper_idx": 62, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_62.txt", "segment_text": "Do it mainly have to do with auto-regressive style versus encoder-decoder (that T0 does not predict the input)?"}, {"seg_id": 545, "paper_idx": 63, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_63.txt", "segment_text": "This contradicts the usual expectation about the EBP, namely that in the (M) case, the model would be \"lost\" in unknown territory and start to produce very poor text."}, {"seg_id": 546, "paper_idx": 63, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_63.txt", "segment_text": "The main conclusion of the authors is that: \"although the mismatch between the data and model prefix distribution exists, it is still in the model\u2019s \u201ccomfortable zone\u201d, and is not large enough induce drastic performance loss during generation\"."}, {"seg_id": 555, "paper_idx": 63, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_63.txt", "segment_text": "That said, the paper makes rather strong claims (e.g., that \u201cperformance gain is minimal\u201d if exposure bias is supposedly eliminated) that I find unfounded given my points in (1) and (2)."}, {"seg_id": 556, "paper_idx": 64, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_64.txt", "segment_text": "I am bit unconvinced about claiming that this work requires no external architecture in Table 1."}, {"seg_id": 562, "paper_idx": 66, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_66.txt", "segment_text": "And when exploring the effect of the architecture, only MLM loss is report."}, {"seg_id": 565, "paper_idx": 66, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_66.txt", "segment_text": "I encourage the authors to also observe downstream performance and note if they agree or disagree with the MLM loss."}, {"seg_id": 571, "paper_idx": 68, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_68.txt", "segment_text": "My biggest concern is on the underlying assumption of this paper---the model should not be impacted by the incorrect labels."}, {"seg_id": 574, "paper_idx": 70, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_70.txt", "segment_text": "The main question I have is: why? I don't think this is spelled out clearly enough in the main paper. Do the authors expect SAP-like approaches to eventually overtake supervised approaches? If so, the questions around computational complexity are central and should be discussed in much more detail in the main section of the paper, including perhaps some FLOPS statistics for the proposed approach and the current SOTA."}, {"seg_id": 583, "paper_idx": 71, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_71.txt", "segment_text": "It would be useful if the authors could report the time during training and compare the latency to the baseline models."}, {"seg_id": 585, "paper_idx": 71, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_71.txt", "segment_text": "The memory/parameter efficiency of LoRA is exaggerated."}, {"seg_id": 589, "paper_idx": 71, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_71.txt", "segment_text": "I would encourage the authors to provide a discussion about the training efficiency."}, {"seg_id": 591, "paper_idx": 71, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_71.txt", "segment_text": "Moreover, with regard to training efficiency, it is better to compare with adapters and prompt/prefix-tuning with the same amount of trainable parameters."}, {"seg_id": 592, "paper_idx": 71, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_71.txt", "segment_text": "The training efficiency of LoRA is a bit unclear."}, {"seg_id": 595, "paper_idx": 73, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_73.txt", "segment_text": "And why were 67 other colors inserted in the color prompts?"}, {"seg_id": 599, "paper_idx": 73, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_73.txt", "segment_text": "This ends up being more a measure of the few-shot learning ability of the model than the conceptual grounding capabilities of the model."}, {"seg_id": 602, "paper_idx": 73, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_73.txt", "segment_text": "For example, in Section 2.1, the authors say that all models are pretrained on the 40GB OPENAI-WT dataset, but this is not true for GPT-3?"}, {"seg_id": 606, "paper_idx": 73, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_73.txt", "segment_text": "Including all n-1 concepts besides the target one, or many such concepts (eg, 60 colors) provides a very strong constraint in the input (in my view contradicting the \"only a small number of examples\" claim in the paper's abstract)."}, {"seg_id": 609, "paper_idx": 74, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_74.txt", "segment_text": "Their approach is an extension of Electra rather than something fundamentally new."}, {"seg_id": 612, "paper_idx": 74, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_74.txt", "segment_text": "1. While the paper claim the importance of language modeling capability of pre-trained models, the authors did not conduct experments on generation tasks that are more likely to require a well-performing language model."}, {"seg_id": 613, "paper_idx": 74, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_74.txt", "segment_text": "What I find missing is that the paper hasn\u2019t addressed the claim about missing language modelling benefit (line 107-113)."}, {"seg_id": 614, "paper_idx": 74, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_74.txt", "segment_text": "Experiments on word similarity and SquAD in section 5.3 cannot really reflect the capability of language modeling."}, {"seg_id": 617, "paper_idx": 74, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_74.txt", "segment_text": "3. The comparison with Megatron is a little overrated."}, {"seg_id": 618, "paper_idx": 74, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_74.txt", "segment_text": "The performance of Megatron and COCO-LM is close to other approaches, for examples, RoBERTa, ELECTRA, and DeBERTa, which are with similar sizes as COCO-LM."}, {"seg_id": 620, "paper_idx": 75, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_75.txt", "segment_text": "While there are clear strengths, one weakness is that one may need to define structural properties in different types of datasets that one might use."}, {"seg_id": 622, "paper_idx": 75, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_75.txt", "segment_text": "Since there is minor improvement in the results, it is difficult to gain confidence that the results will indeed improve on other datasets as well"}, {"seg_id": 623, "paper_idx": 75, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_75.txt", "segment_text": "2. Marginal improvements in results"}, {"seg_id": 624, "paper_idx": 75, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_75.txt", "segment_text": "However, the improvements due to the proposed method are marginal."}, {"seg_id": 625, "paper_idx": 75, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_75.txt", "segment_text": "However, it would have been interesting to see tasks or domains where improvement is more significant."}, {"seg_id": 626, "paper_idx": 75, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_75.txt", "segment_text": "It is unclear how does the method generalise across different tasks and datasets, i.e., beyond two datasets."}, {"seg_id": 627, "paper_idx": 75, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_75.txt", "segment_text": "Using locality info in authors' experiments leads to minor improvement."}, {"seg_id": 630, "paper_idx": 76, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_76.txt", "segment_text": "However, the contribution in terms of the technical novelty is not much. [1] The Algorithmic Foundations of Differential Privacy, 2014. [2] Differentially Private Language Models Benefit from Public Pre-training.2020."}, {"seg_id": 634, "paper_idx": 77, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_77.txt", "segment_text": "How can you improve the model to link rare entities not in the Wikipedia pages?"}, {"seg_id": 635, "paper_idx": 77, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_77.txt", "segment_text": "would it do better on popular entities or more long tail ones?"}, {"seg_id": 637, "paper_idx": 78, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_78.txt", "segment_text": "Weak."}, {"seg_id": 639, "paper_idx": 78, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_78.txt", "segment_text": "The experiment results are relatively weak."}, {"seg_id": 640, "paper_idx": 78, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_78.txt", "segment_text": "However, the experiment part is relatively weak and cannot prove the empirical contribution of this paper."}, {"seg_id": 645, "paper_idx": 79, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_79.txt", "segment_text": "The techniques seem highly tuned for mining well-organized data sets from coding-problem websites."}, {"seg_id": 649, "paper_idx": 79, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_79.txt", "segment_text": "The method and data set in this paper in its current form are rather trivial and more sophistication is required."}, {"seg_id": 655, "paper_idx": 80, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_80.txt", "segment_text": "The technical contribution is incremental, without significant novelty."}, {"seg_id": 656, "paper_idx": 80, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_80.txt", "segment_text": "It reads like the most significant contribution is using Google search as the information retriever compared with previous work using other search engines on Wikipedia, etc."}, {"seg_id": 658, "paper_idx": 80, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_80.txt", "segment_text": "Another concern regarding the paper that I have is that most of the results are pretty well-known - e.g. performance of open-book models are better than parametric models, and Google search is better."}, {"seg_id": 659, "paper_idx": 80, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_80.txt", "segment_text": "Only google search results are used for retrieval."}, {"seg_id": 663, "paper_idx": 81, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_81.txt", "segment_text": "However, only a two-phase setting is considered."}, {"seg_id": 666, "paper_idx": 81, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_81.txt", "segment_text": "The experimental findings in this paper are somewhat trivial."}, {"seg_id": 681, "paper_idx": 84, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_84.txt", "segment_text": "Qualitative analysis - Given that it is a generative paper, I'd like to see examples of how the generated texts look like for different labels. How different are those from the ones present in the training sample?"}, {"seg_id": 684, "paper_idx": 84, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_84.txt", "segment_text": "Susceptibility to hyper-parameter tuning - The optimization procedure is performing three interleaved gradient updates at every iteration."}, {"seg_id": 685, "paper_idx": 84, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_84.txt", "segment_text": "Besides, it is well known that meta-learning methods are notoriously hard to tune and can be extremely sensitive to hyperparameter choices. It is hard to tune the framework on a large PLM with a small batch size."}, {"seg_id": 698, "paper_idx": 84, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_84.txt", "segment_text": "The paper does not conduct quantitative and qualitative analysis to measure the quality of the generated text."}, {"seg_id": 701, "paper_idx": 85, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_85.txt", "segment_text": "1, The paper is weak on ablations."}, {"seg_id": 702, "paper_idx": 85, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_85.txt", "segment_text": "Is there any ablation result showing the necessity of generalist (no external memory)? How does the performance vary if the generalist is absent?"}, {"seg_id": 706, "paper_idx": 85, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_85.txt", "segment_text": "The paper lacks ablation studies to investigate the effectiveness of each component of the proposed method."}, {"seg_id": 708, "paper_idx": 86, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_86.txt", "segment_text": "Tab 1b is confusing."}, {"seg_id": 709, "paper_idx": 86, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_86.txt", "segment_text": "I don't understand what each number means."}, {"seg_id": 712, "paper_idx": 86, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_86.txt", "segment_text": "Prototype quality: prototypes do not look very convincing."}, {"seg_id": 716, "paper_idx": 86, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_86.txt", "segment_text": "Hyperparameters: My initial impression of the method is that it involves far too many hyperparameters (various $\\lambda$ for combining losses, choosing similarity metrics etc)."}, {"seg_id": 717, "paper_idx": 86, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_86.txt", "segment_text": "According to the illustrations at the end of Section 3.4, there are many hyper-parameters should be set (along with lambda terms in training loss)."}, {"seg_id": 719, "paper_idx": 86, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_86.txt", "segment_text": "If we move to a new dataset, we might lost in tuning these hyperparams."}, {"seg_id": 720, "paper_idx": 88, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_88.txt", "segment_text": "(5).As a MoE model, it is basically necessary to control the number of flos and compare it with the dense models and sparse models with the same number of flops, but this paper does not report total training flops number and total train computer (PF-days)."}, {"seg_id": 723, "paper_idx": 89, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_89.txt", "segment_text": "Also, retrieval augmented LMs often have lesser parameters [5], so it's unclear which is a better method from an efficiency perspective."}, {"seg_id": 726, "paper_idx": 89, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_89.txt", "segment_text": "The paper would be much stronger with experiments on GPT3, Instruct-GPT3 (davinci-002), and larger language models (larger in-house LMs?)."}, {"seg_id": 727, "paper_idx": 89, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_89.txt", "segment_text": "It's not really clear from the paper whether recitation helps with larger scale, which I think is important for the generalizability of the method [1]."}, {"seg_id": 728, "paper_idx": 89, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_89.txt", "segment_text": "what's the performance of larger LM (PaLM-500B or GPT-3/Instruct-GPT3) or smaller (GPT-J 6B)?"}, {"seg_id": 733, "paper_idx": 89, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_89.txt", "segment_text": "Note that I do think it's incredible that closed book LMs are doing so well, but practically (from a performance stand-point) it may be better to just retrieve some text from the web rather than ask an LM to generate it with few-shot examples."}, {"seg_id": 735, "paper_idx": 90, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_90.txt", "segment_text": "The constructed test sample size for the benchmark is a bit small (250 samples)."}, {"seg_id": 738, "paper_idx": 91, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_91.txt", "segment_text": "Experimentation could be greatly improved: no hyperparameter search or reasoning behind the selection, no baselines with other memory architectures showing differences either performance or specific use-cases (beyond plain transformers)."}, {"seg_id": 744, "paper_idx": 91, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_91.txt", "segment_text": "Besides the LM task, it could be better if more document-level downstream tasks could be evaluated to verify the usefulness of the proposed approach."}, {"seg_id": 746, "paper_idx": 91, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_91.txt", "segment_text": "The method is not compared with previous models in the experiments, such as Transformer-XL, Compressive Transformers, and other efficient Transformers for LM."}, {"seg_id": 747, "paper_idx": 91, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_91.txt", "segment_text": "Throughout the paper the authors only compare their model to baselines that they designed and trained."}, {"seg_id": 748, "paper_idx": 91, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_91.txt", "segment_text": "Doing this for some of the datasets is fine, but not even having one dataset in which you compare to strong, previously published models makes it harder to convince the reader that you have found something interesting."}, {"seg_id": 749, "paper_idx": 91, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_91.txt", "segment_text": "You did show results for PG19, which has been used before in LMing papers, so it\u2019s not clear why you haven\u2019t compared to previously published models on that dataset, such as the compressive transformer?"}, {"seg_id": 751, "paper_idx": 91, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_91.txt", "segment_text": "The authors don\u2019t provide any speed or memory usage statistics."}, {"seg_id": 755, "paper_idx": 91, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_91.txt", "segment_text": "The authors do not compare their model to any previously published model."}, {"seg_id": 756, "paper_idx": 91, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_91.txt", "segment_text": "The results on PG-19 doesn't seem to replicate previous work (original paper shows 33.6 for Compressive transformer, whereas this work shows ~19 for plain transformers)."}, {"seg_id": 757, "paper_idx": 91, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_91.txt", "segment_text": "Results are very close for each memory size and there are no error measurements (e.g., std dev)."}, {"seg_id": 759, "paper_idx": 91, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_91.txt", "segment_text": "For example, on the arxiv dataset the authors talk about a drop from 2.927 to 2.877 perplexity between two different models, but is a 0.05 perplexity difference really statistically significant here?"}, {"seg_id": 761, "paper_idx": 93, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_93.txt", "segment_text": "Two out of the three tasks are generation tasks in which BLEU or ROUGE-2 evaluation scores are difficult to interpret, i.e. it is very difficult to judge the effectiveness of the proposed method."}, {"seg_id": 763, "paper_idx": 93, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_93.txt", "segment_text": "Furthermore, it is not clear where the results are significant different among systems."}, {"seg_id": 766, "paper_idx": 94, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_94.txt", "segment_text": "[1] elaborates on the few problems with CrowS-Pairs and StereoSet."}, {"seg_id": 774, "paper_idx": 95, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_95.txt", "segment_text": "There is also no discussion on label distribution in the vote-k process."}, {"seg_id": 775, "paper_idx": 95, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_95.txt", "segment_text": "Since the selective annotation is based entirely on similarities derived from sentence embeddings, there is nothing explicit ensuring that the label distribution over the selected subset is not skewed."}, {"seg_id": 779, "paper_idx": 95, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_95.txt", "segment_text": "But novelty here is (1) in combining these ideas to make a practical system, and (2) the experimental work demonstrating some insights comparing this with fine-tuning and other selective annotation schemes."}, {"seg_id": 781, "paper_idx": 96, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_96.txt", "segment_text": "The gains are not super high."}, {"seg_id": 787, "paper_idx": 96, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_96.txt", "segment_text": "3. The experimental results are weak."}, {"seg_id": 788, "paper_idx": 96, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_96.txt", "segment_text": "The authors only conduct the evaluation on sentence similarity tasks and open domain QA tasks."}, {"seg_id": 789, "paper_idx": 96, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_96.txt", "segment_text": "I will be more convinced if evaluation is done on a wider range of tasks."}, {"seg_id": 790, "paper_idx": 96, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_96.txt", "segment_text": "For example, sentence inference tasks such as MNLI and RTE are common tasks in NLP field."}, {"seg_id": 791, "paper_idx": 96, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_96.txt", "segment_text": "The authors should conduct experiments on more types of sentence pair tasks."}, {"seg_id": 792, "paper_idx": 96, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_96.txt", "segment_text": "3.The experiments are limited."}, {"seg_id": 793, "paper_idx": 96, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_96.txt", "segment_text": "I felt like the empirical validation could have been stronger."}, {"seg_id": 797, "paper_idx": 97, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_97.txt", "segment_text": "The authors should answer the question: how could the paper findings benefit the community?"}, {"seg_id": 798, "paper_idx": 98, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_98.txt", "segment_text": "The idea of using entity abstraction is straightforward and not very novel as doing POS tagging for input is widely used in many NLP models, especially in traditional NLP pipelines."}, {"seg_id": 799, "paper_idx": 98, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_98.txt", "segment_text": "While the performance on synthetic datasets is promising, on realistic datasets the proposed method fails to improve the performance."}, {"seg_id": 802, "paper_idx": 98, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_98.txt", "segment_text": "There is very minor boost in performance on QA datasets."}, {"seg_id": 803, "paper_idx": 98, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_98.txt", "segment_text": "The goal of the proposed model should be improving real-world data, instead of synthetic data (CLUTTR in this paper)."}, {"seg_id": 804, "paper_idx": 98, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_98.txt", "segment_text": "Because improving numbers on synthetic data could be hacky, and not scalable to practical applications."}, {"seg_id": 807, "paper_idx": 98, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_98.txt", "segment_text": "The paper only shows end-to-end performance on the three tasks without deeper analysis on why entity abstraction does not always improve the performance."}, {"seg_id": 808, "paper_idx": 98, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_98.txt", "segment_text": "For empirical results, the only significant improvement is on a synthetic dataset while the gain on real-world datasets is also marginal."}, {"seg_id": 809, "paper_idx": 98, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_98.txt", "segment_text": "Only one pretrained transformer model t5-small is tested, therefore the empirical findings of this paper cannot be applied to other models, which usually contain much more parameters and perform better on various downstream tasks."}, {"seg_id": 812, "paper_idx": 98, "paper_path": "/home/wg25r/split_review_opus_repro/meta/ReviewCritique/scripts/subagent_workflows/human_overlap/papers/paper_98.txt", "segment_text": "The method of incorporating abstraction is not universal."}]

log(`Judging ${TASKS.length} overlapped human weakness segments (revised guideline)`)

function buildPrompt(t) {
  return `You are an expert NLP/ML conference meta-reviewer. Judge whether ONE specific weakness/concern segment from a human review is reliable, USING THE PAPER TEXT as ground truth.

Error type table:
${ERROR_TYPE_TABLE}

Weakness reliability guideline (error type table + human-annotated examples of BOTH unreliable and reliable weaknesses):
${GUIDELINE}

First, use the Read tool to read the full paper text at: ${t.paper_path}

The SPECIFIC segment to judge:
=== SEGMENT START ===
${t.segment_text}
=== SEGMENT END ===

Task:
Judge whether THIS SPECIFIC segment is reliable BY CHECKING IT AGAINST THE PAPER TEXT.

Return:
- reliable: 1 if this segment is a genuine, well-grounded criticism of the paper (the flaw really exists, the omission is real, the concern is legitimate). 0 ONLY if it clearly matches one of the error patterns in the guideline (Misunderstanding/Neglect/Vague/Out-of-scope/etc.).
- error_type: if reliable=0, choose the single best-matching label from the error type table (exact label text). If reliable=1, use an empty string.
- justification: 1-2 sentences citing the specific paper passage.

Do NOT mark a segment unreliable merely because it is a criticism or because you could imagine a rebuttal. Most genuine weaknesses raised by reviewers ARE reliable (see the reliable examples in the guideline). Only mark reliable=0 when the segment clearly matches an error pattern -- e.g. it contradicts something explicitly in the paper, misreads a claim, is vague/generic, or asks for something out of scope. When in doubt, mark reliable=1.

Return your result via the required structured output only.`
}

const CONCURRENCY = 2
const results = []
for (let i = 0; i < TASKS.length; i += CONCURRENCY) {
  const chunk = TASKS.slice(i, i + CONCURRENCY)
  const chunkResults = await parallel(chunk.map((t) => async () => {
    const out = await agent(buildPrompt(t), {
      label: `seg${t.seg_id}_p${t.paper_idx}`,
      schema: SCHEMA,
      model: 'sonnet',
    })
    if (!out) return null
    return {
      seg_id: t.seg_id,
      paper_idx: t.paper_idx,
      segment_text: t.segment_text,
      pred_reliable: out.reliable,
      pred_error_type: out.error_type,
      pred_justification: out.justification,
    }
  }))
  results.push(...chunkResults)
  log(`chunk ${i / CONCURRENCY + 1}/${Math.ceil(TASKS.length / CONCURRENCY)} done`)
}

const flat = results.filter(Boolean)
log(`${flat.length} segments judged`)
return flat
